---

<section id="use-cases" class="relative overflow-hidden bg-slate-900/50 px-6 py-24">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl">
    <div class="max-w-4xl">
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[#F08252]">
        Free WordPress image format conversion
      </p>
      <h2 class="mt-4 text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white md:text-5xl">
        Convert JPG and PNG to WebP or AVIF in WordPress.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-6 text-lg leading-8 text-slate-300">
        Rupanta Media Converter is a free WebP and AVIF converter for WordPress.
        Whether you need a WordPress JPG-to-WebP plugin, an AVIF image converter,
        automatic image conversion on upload or a bulk Media Library converter,
        it helps create modern-format copies of supported JPEG and PNG images
        directly on your server.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 grid gap-5 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
      {useCases.map((useCase) => (
        <article class="rounded-3xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-7">
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold leading-8 text-white">{useCase.title}</h3>
          <p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-slate-400">{useCase.description}</p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 grid gap-8 rounded-4xl border border-[#F08252]/25 bg-slate-950/70 p-8 lg:grid-cols-[1.1fr_0.9fr] lg:p-10">
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white md:text-3xl">
          How to convert WordPress images to WebP or AVIF
        </h3>
        <ol class="mt-6 space-y-4 text-slate-300">
          <li><strong class="text-[#F08252]">1.</strong> Install and activate <a href="https://wordpress.org/plugins/lightweight-modern-media/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="font-semibold text-orange-200 underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-white">Rupanta Media Converter on WordPress.org</a>.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-[#F08252]">2.</strong> Open <strong class="text-white">Media → Rupanta Media Converter</strong> in WordPress admin.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-[#F08252]">3.</strong> Check whether your server supports WebP or AVIF through GD or Imagick and choose your formats and quality settings.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-[#F08252]">4.</strong> Run the batch converter for existing images. New supported uploads can convert automatically.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-[#F08252]">5.</strong> Enable frontend picture output to serve available modern formats alongside an original-image fallback.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <aside class="rounded-3xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-7">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white">
          What this WordPress image converter does — and does not do
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-slate-300">
          Rupanta generates WebP and AVIF versions of supported WordPress images.
          It preserves your originals, supports batch conversion and does not require
          an image-processing API or cloud account.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-slate-400">
          It is not a CDN or a general-purpose image resizing service. Smaller
          image transfers and site-speed improvements can vary by image,
          selected quality, browser and hosting configuration.
        </p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
